    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "executive_summary",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "july_reconciliation",
        "body": "## Executive summary\n\n- **The figures reconcile correctly.** Treating each supplied residual as `actual − forecast`, the reconstructed daily forecasts match the app's Random Forest output within **₹0.50 per day**; the difference is only rounding.\n- **Overall July performance is good, but not yet planning-grade at the daily level.** For July 1–27, actual net sales were **₹14.25M** against **₹13.93M** forecast. WAPE was **12.65%**, MAE was **₹66.77k**, and R² was **0.78**.\n- **The central issue is a mid-month level shift.** July 1–12 was underforecast by **₹900.63k**, while July 13–27 was overforecast by **₹571.47k**. The model captured the broad weekly sales rhythm but could not update after the July actuals moved to a lower level.\n- **Use the forecast as a directional baseline with an operating tolerance.** Fourteen of 27 days were within 10% and 22 were within 20%; five days exceeded 25% absolute error."
      },
      {
        "id": "headline_metrics",
        "type": "metric-strip",
        "cardIds": [
          "actual_total_card",
          "forecast_total_card",
          "wape_card",
          "mae_card",
          "bias_card",
          "r2_card"
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "accuracy_finding",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "july_reconciliation",
        "body": "## Overall accuracy and fit\n\nThe total forecast was only **₹329.16k below actual**, a **2.31%** net miss. That small total bias should not be confused with small daily errors: positive and negative misses partly cancelled, while absolute error still totalled **₹1.80M**. The **0.90 actual-versus-forecast correlation** and **0.78 R²** show that the model followed much of the daily pattern, including stronger weekends, but it smoothed some peaks and did not adapt to the later-month slowdown."
      },
      {
        "id": "actual_forecast_visual",
        "type": "chart",
        "chartId": "actual_forecast_chart"
      },
      {
        "id": "error_pattern_finding",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "july_reconciliation",
        "body": "## Error pattern and exceptions\n\nThe error direction changed materially after July 12. In the first 12 days, **10 of 12** were underforecast; in the following 15 days, **12 of 15** were overforecast. Weekly signed variance moved from **+₹507.33k** and **+₹397.01k** in the first two periods to **−₹397.56k** and **−₹177.62k** in the final two periods. This is evidence of a level shift rather than a stable one-sided bias.\n\nThe largest percentage misses were July 20 (**46.68% overforecast**), July 24 (**38.09% overforecast**), July 16 (**33.87% overforecast**), July 27 (**29.92% overforecast**), and July 1 (**27.94% underforecast**). The two largest actual-sales peaks—July 12 at **₹974.17k** and July 5 at **₹916.87k**—were both underforecast, so peak-demand coverage remains the main inventory and staffing risk."
      },
      {
        "id": "weekly_bias_visual",
        "type": "chart",
        "chartId": "weekly_bias_chart"
      },
      {
        "id": "model_finding",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "model_validation_output",
        "body": "## Model selection and benchmark\n\nThe app selected **Random Forest Regressor** because it had the lowest chronological holdout RMSE among the five candidates. On that holdout it recorded **17.27% WAPE**, **₹94.60k MAE**, and **0.49 R²**. July's out-of-sample results were better—**12.65% WAPE**, **₹66.77k MAE**, and **0.78 R²**—but the windows differ, so this should be read as encouraging evidence rather than proof of a permanent improvement."
      },
      {
        "id": "model_validation_visual",
        "type": "table",
        "tableId": "model_validation_table"
      },
      {
        "id": "operating_recommendations",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "july_reconciliation",
        "body": "## Recommended operating remarks\n\n1. **Keep the current Random Forest as the baseline model.** It outperformed the available alternatives in chronological validation and reproduced the supplied forecast values correctly.\n2. **Refresh the forecast with actuals at least weekly.** A fixed 27-day recursive forecast could not respond to the mid-month change. Reforecasting after July 7 and July 14 would reduce exposure to persistent level shifts.\n3. **Use forecast ranges for daily decisions.** For normal staffing and inventory, plan around roughly **±20%** of the point forecast; 22 of 27 days fell inside that band. Use a wider exception process for Mondays and event/promotion days.\n4. **Create peak-day overrides.** Sundays and other high-volume dates were generally tracked directionally but peak values were understated. Add promotion, holiday, payday, stock availability, and local-event features before using the forecast for replenishment commitments.\n5. **Monitor bias separately from absolute accuracy.** A small monthly net bias can hide large offsetting daily errors. The operating scorecard should show WAPE/MAE together with signed bias and the count of days outside 20%."
      },
      {
        "id": "daily_detail_intro",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "july_reconciliation",
        "body": "## Daily audit detail\n\nThe table below preserves the supplied values exactly. Positive residuals mean actual sales exceeded forecast (underforecast); negative residuals mean the forecast exceeded actual sales (overforecast)."
      },
      {
        "id": "daily_detail_visual",
        "type": "table",
        "tableId": "daily_audit_table"
      },
      {
        "id": "further_questions",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## Further questions to investigate\n\n- Were promotions, holidays, stock-outs, store closures, or unusual weather present on July 1, 16, 20, 24, or 27?\n- Did store mix, section, department, or division mix change after July 12?\n- Would store-level forecasts aggregated to the total outperform a single all-store daily model?\n- How much accuracy improves when actual July values are fed back through weekly rolling retraining?"
      },
      {
        "id": "caveats",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## Caveats and assumptions\n\n- The 27 supplied rows are assumed to correspond in order to **July 1–27, 2026**.\n- `Original − forecast` is treated as **actual net sales minus forecast**. Forecast values in the audit table are reconstructed as `actual − residual`.\n- The historical workbook ends on **June 30, 2026**, so these July values are out-of-sample.\n- The app's interval is a fixed **±1.96 × holdout RMSE** band. All 27 actuals fall inside it, but that band is wide and is not a calibrated probabilistic interval.\n- Daily values supplied as whole rupees introduce tiny rounding differences; the maximum difference from the app's unrounded forecast is below **₹0.50 per day**."
      }
    ]
  },
